# VRYOH — U.S. Automatic-Renewal and Subscription Law Review
**Research date:** October 14, 2026  
**Company:** Solofella LLC (Wyoming)  
**Product:** VRYOH — B2B SaaS  
**Subscription prices:** $30/month/location or $300/year/location  
**Governing law:** New York; courts in Rockland County, NY

## Executive findings

The most important distinction is between **laws protecting individual consumers** and **laws governing contracts between businesses**.

VRYOH is intended exclusively for business customers. Several prominent state automatic-renewal laws expressly define consumers as individuals purchasing for personal, family, or household purposes. That distinction substantially affects which statutory requirements apply.

However, operating B2B does not establish a blanket exemption from every automatic-renewal law.

### Five preliminary conclusions

**1. New York has two materially different automatic-renewal statutes.**

New York General Business Law Article 29-BB addresses consumer subscriptions. Its definition of consumer is limited to individuals purchasing for personal, family, or household purposes.

New York General Obligations Law § 5-903 is broader: it expressly recognizes companies and corporations. However, it applies to specified service, maintenance, or repair contracts involving real or personal property. Whether it covers VRYOH's SaaS subscription requires separate legal analysis. [NYSenate.gov](https://www.nysenate.gov/legislation/laws/GOB/5-903?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

**2. The FTC's 2024 Click-to-Cancel Rule is not currently enforceable.**

The Eighth Circuit vacated that rule on July 8, 2025.

The FTC confirmed the vacatur in its March 2026 rulemaking documents. The earlier, narrower Negative Option Rule remains, alongside other applicable federal laws. [Federal Trade Commission](https://www.ftc.gov/system/files/ftc_gov/pdf/2025-12-Final-Public.pdf?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

**3. The business-customer distinction matters for Florida.**

Florida Statutes § 501.165 expressly excludes individuals entering covered contracts as part of their business activities.

That supports excluding genuine VRYOH business purchases from this particular Florida automatic-renewal statute. It does not exempt them from all Florida contract or deceptive-practices laws. [Online Sunshine](https://www.leg.state.fl.us/Statutes/index.cfm/Ch0466/index.cfm?App_mode=Display_Statute\&URL=0500-0599%2F0501%2FSections%2F0501.165.html\&utm_source=chatgpt.com)

**4. VRYOH's proposed annual-renewal reminder is useful, but Section 5 needs clarification.**

Your promised 30-day annual reminder is a reasonable contractual protection. However, several laws prescribe specific notice windows, and some calculate the window from the **cancellation deadline**, not simply the renewal date.

A 30-day reminder therefore should not be assumed universally compliant.

**5. VRYOH's billing model contains an unresolved contractual question.**

For monthly subscribers joining on any day other than the 25th, the current clause does not explain:

- What the customer pays on signup.
- Whether the initial partial month is prorated.
- Whether the next charge occurs on the upcoming 25th.
- Whether the initial payment covers a full month or only part of one.

This ambiguity is independently worth correcting, even if no state statute dictates a particular proration method.

**Source classification:** The statutory distinctions and FTC vacatur are directly verified from official sources. The assessment of VRYOH's contract and recommended operational practices is analysis, not a judicial determination of applicability.

