# 1. New York automatic-renewal laws

## 1.1 General Business Law §§ 527 and 527-a

**Confidence: HIGH — statutory text verified.**

**Official sources:**

- Definitions: https://www.nysenate.gov/legislation/laws/GBS/527
- Requirements: https://www.nysenate.gov/legislation/laws/GBS/527-A
- Both pages identify their most recent revision as **November 7, 2025**.

The definition is decisive:

> “Consumer” means any individual who seeks or acquires [...] for personal, family, or household purposes.

This is from § 527(3). [NYSenate.gov](https://www.nysenate.gov/legislation/laws/GBS/527?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

### What the law states

For covered consumer subscriptions, § 527-a requires:

| Practice | New York requirement |
|---|---|
| Signup disclosure | Material renewal terms clearly displayed before requesting consent or billing information |
| Consent | Affirmative consent before charging |
| Confirmation | Prompt, retainable notice containing subscription and cancellation terms |
| Cancellation | Simple process, as easy as enrollment, available through the enrollment medium |
| Annual renewal notice | 15–45 days before the cancellation deadline for qualifying subscriptions |
| Price increases | Consent to the increase or a specified cancellation/pro-rata refund protection |
| Material changes | Notice 5 business days to 30 days before the change |
| Free trials | Additional notice requirements for qualifying trials | :chatgpt-content-reference{index="4"}


**Important price-increase distinction:** Your proposed “at least 30 days' notice” clause is not equivalent to New York's consumer rules.

Section 527-a contains two different requirements:

First, under § 527-a(1)(b-1), a covered price increase requires affirmative consent **or** a cancellation opportunity lasting at least 14 days after the charge, with the specified pro-rata refund.

Second, § 527-a(1)(g) specifies a notice window for material changes, including price increases:

> “at least five business days prior, but no more than thirty days prior”

Source: https://www.nysenate.gov/legislation/laws/GBS/527-A

**Conclusion for VRYOH:** Because you sell business-use SaaS, the statute's definition generally excludes those transactions.

**Confidence: HIGH** on the consumer-purpose definition; **MEDIUM** on applying it to every possible VRYOH purchase because actual purchaser identity and use must be considered.

## 1.2 General Obligations Law § 5-903

**Confidence: HIGH** for statutory text; **LOW** for applicability to VRYOH.

**Source:** https://www.nysenate.gov/legislation/laws/GOB/5-903

**Most recent revision shown:** September 22, 2014.

The statute expressly includes commercial entities:

> “person” means an individual, firm, company, partnership or corporation.

For covered contracts, renewal provisions may be unenforceable unless the provider delivers specified written notice.

The statute requires notice:

> “at least fifteen days and not more than thirty days”

before the contractual deadline for giving nonrenewal notice. It specifies personal delivery or certified mail and excludes renewal periods of one month or less. [NYSenate.gov](https://www.nysenate.gov/legislation/laws/GOB/5-903?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

### Does it cover SaaS?

The statute concerns service, maintenance, or repair **to or for real or personal property**.

VRYOH provides hosted software, review analysis, and response drafting.

I could not verify from the statute alone whether these services qualify under § 5-903.

**Conclusion:** Annual B2B renewal applicability is an attorney-review item. Do not assume the consumer-only limitation of § 527-a also applies to § 5-903.

A further issue: if § 5-903 applies, an email reminder alone may not satisfy its specified delivery requirements.

