# 5. Billing dates and mid-month subscriptions

**Confidence: MEDIUM — statutory research did not establish a universal proration rule.**

I did not find an applicable federal or reviewed state automatic-renewal provision prescribing the 25th as an impermissible billing date.

I also could not verify a general rule requiring a SaaS provider to prorate subscriptions when enrollment occurs mid-month.

That does not prove such rules are absent from every state.

### VRYOH's two possible monthly models

**Model A — Fixed billing date**

Every customer renews on the 25th.

Example:

A customer signs up October 10.

The customer receives a prorated initial subscription through October 24, then pays $30 per location on October 25 for the next monthly period.

**Model B — Signup anniversary**

A customer who subscribes October 10 renews November 10, December 10, and so forth.

Every customer receives a full billing period from their initial payment.

### Recommendation

For the system you described, **Model A is the better match**, provided the initial partial period is clearly priced before payment.

This is a business recommendation, **not a statutory requirement**.

A checkout should clearly display:

> Initial prorated charge: $[amount], covering [start date] through [end date]. Your next charge will be $30 per location on [date], with monthly renewal thereafter.

The applicable charge should be calculated and displayed by the actual billing system.

I would not put a fixed proration formula into Section 5 until you have decided whether the system will use calendar days, fixed 30-day periods, or another disclosed method.

